            <div xml:id="d118">

                <pb facs="0117" n="109" sameAs="frus:frus1941v07/pg109"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.20/174</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador
                        in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Messersmith</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-07-14">July 14, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear George</hi>: Upon receipt of your letter of June
                        5,<anchor n="18" xml:id="d118-f1"/> the attention of the War and Navy
                    Departments was again called to the possibility of sending a joint
                    military-naval mission to Cuba. The Navy Department has just replied that it
                    will not object to such a mission and concurs in your recommendation that, prior
                    to taking any further action, the Cuban Government’s views should be obtained in
                    order to determine the scope of the activities and the functions to be performed
                    by the mission.</p>
                <p>The War Department also expresses its agreement with your recommendation and has
                    voiced no objection in principle to the plan. I think that we can properly
                    assume, therefore, that if the question of establishing such a mission should be
                    proposed again by the Cubans, you can give consideration to the matter in the
                    knowledge that such a suggestion will not be opposed by the military or naval
                    authorities in Washington.</p>
                <p>Believe me [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sumner Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
